div.ab:
After these (things) the Messiah calls beautiful her who believed in him because of the faith and the discipline;
likewise companion because she kept aloof from that which was contrary and drew near to him by the faith, the baptism which is of him, being sanctified by the spirit which in the form of a dove descended upon the Lord, and she came to have spiritual eyes by which she is able to contemplate the sun of righteousness, the Messiah. (v.15) Behold, thou art beautiful, my companion; behold thou art beautiful; thine eyes are doves, that is, it is again possible for these eyes to be understood as spiritual gifts like those which Paul enumerates: for to one is given through the Spirit the spirit of wisdom and so forth (1 Cor. 12:8). Again thine eyes are doves, that is, the apostles and the teachers through whom you see the spiritual things are doves, because they heard the Lord who said: Be cunning like serpents and innocent like the doves, (Math. 10:16), since they became wise by the holy spirit and were made rich by all its gifts. But she replies to him and says: (v.16) Behold thou art beautiful, my sister's son, and fair, that is, when I considered all these things which are of the world and worldly riches, possessions, and wisdom and so forth, I did not find anything more beautiful than you, because I had been hateful, but when I came near to you, you were beautiful and I became beautiful. To our shady bed, that is, where we are at rest with you in the bed, as you have been likened to in the gospel; there it is you who gives us shade and in your shade we delight and rejoice; and if it happen that while being in the world we are sometimes at rest from evil, it is you who gives us shade. Again, you are a shelter to us from the severity of the temptations of the conceivable sun.
(v.17) The beams of our houses are of cedar, that is, our houses; that is to say, our bodies. That by which we are rejuvenated so that they (the bodies) will not suffer harm from the weakness of the shameful desires and of the fire of the foul passions, are the praiseworthy disciplines of virtue, which she calls cedars because of the soundness and endurance of these. Our panelling is of cypresses, that is, the virtuous disciplines of the faith which are visible in us and the manifestations which come to men; they are cypresses because they are not visible on account of anything corruptible, that is, the vain splendor of men, but for the fulfilment of that which is said: That they might see your good works and glorify your father that is in heaven (Math. 5:16); for the cypress is not prominent, so also it is unacceptable to the soul that was made firm by the knowledge of God to do good (with the sole purpose) that her good works should be seen by men.
